{{also|Metamorphose|métamorphosé|métamorphose}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|fr|métamorphoser}}, from {{der|en|fro|metamorphose}}, from {{der|en|la|metamorphōsis}}; see {{m|en|metamorphosis}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌmɛtəˈmɔːˌfəʊz/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-metamorphose.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌmɛɾəˈmɔɹˌfoʊz/|/ˌmɛɾəˈmɔɹˌfoʊs/|a=GA}}
* {{hyph|en|met|a|mor|phose}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|of a moth or insect|intransitive}} To undergo [[metamorphosis]].
# {{lb|en|by extension|intransitive}} To undergo some [[transformation]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1936|author=w:William Faulkner|title=w:Absalom, Absalom!|passage=In fact, perhaps this is the pure and perfect incest: the brother realising that the sister’s virginity must be destroyed in order to have existed at all, taking that virginity in the person of the brother-in-law, the man whom he would be if he could become, '''metamorphose''' into, the lover, the husband; by whom he would be despoiled, choose for despoiler, if the could become, '''metamorphose''' into the sister, the mistress, the bride.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1995|author=Ian Robottom; Hart Paul|title=Behaviorist EE Research: Environmentalism as Individualism|url=http://psycnet.apa.org/record/1995-42290-001|journal=The Journal of Environmental Education|volume=26|number=2|doi=10.1080/00958964.1995.9941433|page=8|passage=Environmental problems are not objectively existing physical phenomena amenable to reliable analysis and diagnosis. They are social constructions whose meaning and significance '''metamorphose''' and wax and wane according to changeable human interest.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1995 January|author=Cullen Murphy|title=In Praise of Snow|journal=w:The Atlantic|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/magazine/archive/1995/01/in-praise-of-snow/305654/|passage=In fresh snow air can pass with little obstruction from the atmosphere through the snowpack to the ground: given life by differences in the temperature gradient, the snowpack breathes. But time changes that. The snow may '''metamorphose''' into what is called [[equitemperature]], or ET, snow.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[transform]] (something) so that it has a completely [[different]] [[appearance]].

